  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Performance\n\nCalls no longer allocate to find their setup (#376, closes #371). Looking up the matching setup used to enumerate a concurrent stack, which allocated on every call. Setups now live in a SetupCollection\\<T> that's enumerated without allocating, and the newest matching setup still wins. Bytes per call:\n\n| Call | Before | After |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| method | 144 B | 96 B |\n| void method | 136 B | 88 B |\n| indexer getter | 104 B | 56 B |\n| generic method (1 / 8 setups) | 312 / 984 B | 168 / 504 B |\n\nSetupCollection\\<T> is a new public type in Imposter.Abstractions, for generated code only. It's hidden from IntelliSense.\n\nLess generator work on every edit (#377, closes #370). The generator now chooses each type parameter's generated name once per symbol. It writes types with no renamed type parameter directly. This drops the allocations of the incremental run after an edit by 20% for plain targets and by half for generic ones, and the generated code is unchanged.\n\nMaintenance\n\nThe README and the docs show a benchmark table that a new workflow regenerates on a GitHub-hosted runner after every change to master (#380, closes #379). The docs page no longer labels Moq's results as Imposter's.\n\nA generator benchmark (benchmarks/Imposter.GeneratorBenchmarks) measures cold and incremental runs (#377, #382, closes #374).",
